// The @std bodied members: the migrated declarations whose bodies are
// plain ds source over word-shaped atoms, the one shape a linked call
// surface binds (a @std bodied member binds only in word-shaped ambient
// atom signatures) and the one a session composes back beside the
// shrunk core — the bodies lower as ambient bodies, where a data member
// (a type or a const) cannot, so the bare spellings keep resolving and
// running for every entry script session while its data-shaped @std
// imports stay bound through the linked surface. The ordering pair is
// the first family (the library-boundary round): the float and
// ord-generic spellings ride the later linked-surface rounds.
//
// The FP combinator family: the list transforms as plain generic
// library fns. A generic body stages values only through the shapes it
// can lower — tuples over the variable (a static word span) and the
// native-backed seams — never through a bare type-variable word: a var
// binding, a cons call, or a member push over the variable has no
// static runtime kind, and an indexed or first read answers a union
// member over the variable, all refusing at lowering. The builders
// append through the concat member over a one-element literal, never
// the push member: a dot-call's free tier prefers the flat name first,
// so a user's private `push` declaration shadows the member call inside
// these bodies and trips the file privacy refusal. zip pairs by the
// nested scan whose inner guard is a catchall match (an `if` guarding a
// type-variable-carrying write refuses at the branch reconcile; the
// match spelling lowers). take, drop, flat_map, scan, and compose wait
// behind the seams above (their residuals ride todo item 81); take,
// drop, and flat_map are unblocked by the plan-time mint seam and wait
// on the library round's phasing alone.
//
// The race combinators over the timer capability: the caller builds the
// thunk list (non-capturing scalar lambdas alone — a closure cannot
// inhabit a collection element), and one spawned child per index finds
// its own thunk by a linear scan whose guard is the int match (the
// quadratic zip scan's own spelling): a spawned body cannot capture a
// callable binding, a call through a call result is a computed callee,
// a callable word cannot ride a channel payload (the send's element
// proof refuses an unproven callable), the member `get` is shadowable
// by a user's private declaration (the dot-call's free tier prefers
// the flat name first, the file privacy refusal), and the private
// `list_get` seam is cross-file inside the @std package — so the
// iteration binding, held and called inside the child's own frame
// alone, is the one shape that composes everywhere — racing the answer
// into the bounded answers channel; the join-all boundary drains after every
// child completed — the channel's FIFO is the arrival order.
// `firstof` answers the first arrival; `interleave` collects in
// arrival order. The empty race traps the channel's zero-bound
// refusal, and a wider-than-one-word answer refuses at the call's
// one-word instantiation. `sum` is the fold spelling over reduce's
// hosted callable (the CALLG lambda seam).
//
// The pooled pair: par_map/par_reduce are library members over the
// fork-join engine seams, one private bodiless generic extern each
// (fork_map/fork_reduce, the native the engine routes by name — the
// same engine-identity key the collection externs in the core hold)
// whose one-line bodies pass their arguments through, so the pooled
// CALLG dispatch rides inside the library body and the sequential
// contract and the byte-identical pool answers carry over unchanged.
// The free generic body's one-word instantiation boundary binds the
// fold's init parameter to one-word element types through this
// spelling (the multi-word fold keeps its seam-level coverage until the
// generic-body staging seam widens, todo item 81's residuals).
pub fn max (a: int, b: int) -> int {
  if a < b { b } else { a }
}
pub fn min (a: int, b: int) -> int {
  if a < b { a } else { b }
}
pub fn enumerate[t] (xs: list[t]) -> list[(int, t)] {
  var out = list[(int, t)]()
  var i: int = 0
  for x in xs {
    out = out.concat([(i, x)])
    i += 1
  }
  out
}
pub fn sum (xs: list[int]) -> int {
  xs.reduce(0, (a: int, x: int) => a + x)
}
pub fn firstof[t] (thunks: list[fn() -> t]) -> t {
  const answers = chan[t](len(thunks))
  nursery {
    for i in 0..len(thunks) {
      spawn {
        var seen: int = 0
        for x in thunks {
          match seen - i {
            0 => { answers.send(x()) }
            _ => {}
          }
          seen += 1
        }
      }
    }
  }
  answers.recv()
}
pub fn interleave[t] (thunks: list[fn() -> t]) -> list[t] {
  const answers = chan[t](len(thunks))
  nursery {
    for i in 0..len(thunks) {
      spawn {
        var seen: int = 0
        for x in thunks {
          match seen - i {
            0 => { answers.send(x()) }
            _ => {}
          }
          seen += 1
        }
      }
    }
  }
  var out = list[t]()
  var j = 0
  while j < len(thunks) {
    out = out.concat([answers.recv()])
    j += 1
  }
  out
}
pub fn zip[t, r] (xs: list[t], ys: list[r]) -> list[(t, r)] {
  var out = list[(t, r)]()
  var i: int = 0
  for x in xs {
    var j: int = 0
    for y in ys {
      match i - j {
        0 => { out = out.concat([(x, y)]) }
        _ => {}
      }
      j += 1
    }
    i += 1
  }
  out
}
extern fn fork_map[t, r] (xs: list[t], f: fn(t) -> r) -> list[r]
extern fn fork_reduce[t] (xs: list[t], init: t, f: fn(t, t) -> t) -> t
pub fn par_map[t, r] (xs: list[t], f: fn(t) -> r) -> list[r] {
  fork_map(xs, f)
}
pub fn par_reduce[t] (xs: list[t], init: t, f: fn(t, t) -> t) -> t {
  fork_reduce(xs, init, f)
}
